<h2>The transaction and the objection</h2>

<p>A cash-credit facility went bad, the bank issued its demand notice for ₹88,52,741, and the secured asset went to auction in April 2009 with an earnest money requirement of ₹21,50,000. The successful bidders tendered ₹21,15,000 — thirty-five thousand rupees short — but deposited a further ₹33,20,000 the same day, satisfying the requirement of Rule 9(3) of the Security Interest (Enforcement) Rules, 2002 that twenty-five per cent of the sale price be paid immediately. The borrower attacked the sale on the EMD shortfall, and the litigation consumed the better part of two decades before reaching final resolution.</p>

<h2>The Court\'s reasoning</h2>

<p>The Bench located the EMD correctly in the scheme: it is a term of the sale notice, not of the statute, and exists to screen out speculative bidders. The legally operative deposits are those in Rules 9(3) and 9(4). Once the purchasers had paid the full quarter of the sale price on auction day, the screening purpose stood served, and the shortfall became an arithmetical footnote. The Court applied the settled tender-law principle that a non-conformity or relaxation which causes no substantial prejudice or injustice to any participant does not invalidate the process — no rival bidder was edged out by the ₹35,000, and the borrower\'s interest lay in price, which was realised.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>The judgment is equally significant for what it required of the bank. Surplus sale proceeds belong to the borrower; the bank\'s failure to place ₹1.33 crores of surplus in an interest-bearing account attracted a refund direction with 7% interest running from 2010. Secured creditors who sit on surpluses should read the direction as a standing warning.</p>
</div>

<h2>The deposit architecture in SARFAESI sales</h2>

<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Stage</th><th>Requirement</th><th>Character</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Bid submission</td><td>Earnest money as fixed by the sale notice</td><td>Contractual/ancillary — filters serious bidders</td></tr>
  <tr><td>On acceptance of bid</td><td>25% of sale price (adjusting EMD) — Rule 9(3)</td><td>Mandatory; default forfeits and reopens sale</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Within the prescribed period</td><td>Balance 75% — Rule 9(4), extendable by written agreement</td><td>Mandatory; default forfeits deposits</td></tr>
  <tr><td>After realisation</td><td>Appropriation per Section 13(7); surplus to the borrower</td><td>Statutory trust — interest-bearing custody expected</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What it means for stakeholders</h2>

<p>For borrowers, the decision narrows the field of procedural challenge: attacks must show real prejudice — undervaluation, exclusion of bidders, notice defects that mattered — not decimal-point deviations. For banks and their authorised officers, the two directions pull together: conduct sales that substantially honour the notified terms, and account for every rupee of the proceeds as a fiduciary would. For auction purchasers, nearly two decades of litigation over ₹35,000 is the cautionary tale itself — comply exactly, document everything, and the law\'s substance-over-form protection becomes a shield rather than a necessity.</p>

